nd mechanics are meant to be  simple for beginners compared to the far  more complex weapons like the charged  blade or gun lands which require hours  to get the hang of along with trips to  outside resources like video tutorials  and the wiki seriously you'll probably  actually want to spend a lot of time on  that wiki but the great thing about  weapons not being tied to characters  stat  is there's no reason you can't just  switch to a completely different weapon  30 hours in if you're looking to try  something a little more complex Monster  Hunter really tries to capture the feel  of being a hunter which means there's a  lot of preparatory rituals you'll want  to do before every hunt you want to buy  and craft basic resources like potions  make sure your armor and weapons are up  to snuff and visit the canteen for a  nice meal bonus the early stages of the  game aren't too demanding that you won't  need to think too hard and specific as  long as you have a decent supply of  basic items you'll be fine  you don't need to get too picky with  armor as long as the defense rating is  high and you'll always be fine picking  the chef's choice option at the canteen  which tailors the meal to the quest the  wait game is rigged you're gonna want to  start thinking a little more  strategically with monsters that will  kick your ass if you're not prepared the  monster field guide gives you all sorts  of important information like what kinds  of attacks the monster does and what  elements and ailments it's weak to  bringing more specific items like  antidotes and null berries to counter  effects becomes necessary picking a  weapon with the right element and  picking armor with the right defense  towards elements is more crucial so  another great thing to do is make  loadouts it's easy to miss but you have  the ability to create specific loadouts  for equipment items and meals I find  creating and then naming a loadout after  a particular monster cuts out a lot of  that prep time when returning from a  hunt there's also numerous shops and  stations back at the hub world of astera  that you'll want to remember to check in  on always check in with the ecological  research field to add more data to your  moscar field guide you'll want to check  in with the resource center to turn in  any completed bounties and always make  sure you're registered bounties list is  full bounties after all are the main way  you obtain armors fears which you need  for upgrading don't forget to collect  your harvest from the botanical Research  Center before the harvest box gets full  by the way if you don't have anything  specific you want to cultivate you can't  go wrong with honey which lets you  easily stockpile mega potions there may  not be much reason to visit your room at  the beginning of the game which made me  kind of forget it was there but once you  unlock the tale riders Safari you can  access it from there so remember to  start visiting so you can always be  sending out pala codes and collecting  rewards okay now let's get to some more  specific tips the Mille boost is great  because it gives you a sizeable boost to  health and stamina but if you faint  during a hunt you lose that boost which  makes the rest of the fight a lot  tougher and can kind of be demoralizing  you can regain back your health boost  though through nutrients so it always  helps to have some on hand for getting  back stamina you can use rations or cook  some meat with this cute little minigame  just don't mess up the timing or you'll  burn your food and the boost won't be as  strong here's a hot tip if you have  items you always want to craft like  nutrients toggle Auto crafting and the  game will make it for you whenever you  have the materials of course when you're  out hunting your prey you'll want to  improvise and use your surroundings  creatures like para toads shoot a mist  of paralysis when hit and flash bugs can  blind nearby enemies just be careful  because you can be hit as well there's  also the environment itself leaping off  a Ledge gives you an opportunity to  mount a monster for high damage certain  walls like ones with giant mushrooms can  act as free boards for cool attacks  there are tons of ways to use the  environment to your advantage and make  your hunts easier  hunting down monsters so you can make  some sweet pants is a lot of the drive  in Monster Hunter so make sure to take  advantage of the wish list and put any  gear you want on it the wish list will  break down exactly what materials you  need and will even hint where to find  them you'll get handy notifications when  you found a material for a piece of gear  and when it's ready to build saving you  a lot of trouble of checking in at the  Smith every time with each area of the  game you'll start with one campsite and  throughout the story you'll get access  to a second one however there are a  total of four in each area and it's up  to you to find the rest you can search  for yourself or you can cheese it and  join a quest with another player who has  already found one and then you in turn  will find it after that you'll get a  special bounty you need to complete and  then the camp is yours throughout the  story you'll be given a handful of  optional quest and while you'll  undoubtedly do some of them I want to  stress that it's within your benefit to  do most if not all of them a lot of  these quests get you fantastic rewards  like new items to cultivate at the  botanical Research Center or new  equipment like Mantle's even the  terrible terrible egg quests are worth  doing for the new canteen ingredients  not all of these quests instantly appear  at the quest board some of them you'll  have to find NPCs with an exclamation  point so keep an eye out for these when  walking around hope you find some of  these tips and tricks helpful for you  Monster Hunter newbies if you're a  monster hunter expert watching this  video you undoubtedly have dozens of  tricks 